  "title": "A Long Cloud on Mars",
  "date": "2026-11-02",
  "year": "2026",
  "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> What\u2019s causing this unusually long cloud on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/\">Mars</a>?\u00a0Known as the <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express/Mars_s_oddest_cloud_may_be_even_odder_than_we_thought\">Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud</a> (AMEC) and first recorded by NASA\u2019s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-2/\">Viking Orbiter 2</a> in 1977, the <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/marss-most-mysterious-cloud-might-be-defying-textbook-cloud-physics\">strange cloud</a> has been imaged in greater detail more recently by <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>\u2019s robotic <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Mars_Express\">Mars Express</a> spacecraft.\u00a0The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/132160802@N06/49046308893/\">featured</a> <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/10/The_Arsia_Mons_Elongated_Cloud_as_seen_by_Mars_Express\">image</a> is from 2018. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud forms</a> only during <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-20-spring-equinox-at-teide-observatory/\">spring</a> and summer <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/seasons/\">seasons</a> and then only during morning hours. Made of water-ice, the most familiar process that causes <a href=\"https://airbornescience.nasa.gov/content/Sampling_the_composition_of_cirrus_ice_residuals\">cloud crystals</a> to <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10387\">form around bits of dust</a> would fail due to the thinness and lack of dust high in the <a href=\"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/mars-atmosphere-equation-english/\">Martian atmosphere</a>. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-026-02089-9\">Leading speculation</a> holds that dust is not involved. After the cloud forms near the volcano <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsia_Mons\">Arsia Mons</a>, wind blows the cloud <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2012-april-22-flowing-barchan-sand-dunes-on-mars/\">downstream</a> \u2014 creating a tail that can extend over 1500 kilometers. Efforts to better understand <a href=\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2020JE006517\">AMEC</a>s will continue, an understanding that may bolster a <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">more complete view</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/\">cloud formation</a> on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> eyeing a cat",
